    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Nissan Returns to the Annual SEMA Show Featuring a 300-HP Sentra Spec-V Revival Concept, Two Z Cars\nThe 2027 Car of the Year Finalists Are Here. Not a Single Exciting Model Among Them!\n8 photos\nPhoto: European Car of the Year\nThe journey to the 2027 European Car of the Year continues with seven vehicles that will battle each other in the grand finale, and each one has an equal shot of driving home with the much-desired crown on its head.\nThe jury recently revealed the seven finalists for the 2027 edition of the European Car of the Year award, and the names that have made it on the list are, in alphabetical order, the BMW iX3, Cupra Raval, Hyundai Ioniq 3, Kia EV2, Renault Twingo, Volkswagen ID. Polo, and Volvo EX60. According to the jurors, “many others deserved a place on the shortlist,” but it is these that will go to the grand finale.\nAs you have likely noticed, the seven rides mentioned above are all EVs, and the organization noted that these fit “ perfectly with the broader trend in the European market , ” while also mentioning the “ fuel crisis triggered by the war in Iran, ” which has “ driven an increase in the number of electric car users. ” This is the first time in the award’s 63-year history when all the finalists are electric vehicles.\nHowever, it won’t be a first win for an EV , because in case you forgot, Renault scored the award in 2024 and 2025 with the Scenic E-Tech and the 5 E-Tech, respectively, whereas the third-generation Mercedes-Benz CLA won last year’s edition. The latter entered production in mid-2025, and it is available not only as a hybrid, but also an electric vehicle, and it shares its MMA (Mercedes-Benz Modular Architecture) with the latest GLA and GLB.\nPhoto: European Car of the Year In order to further trim down the list, all members of the jury will reunite at the Balocco racetrack in Italy toward the end of next month, on November 25 and 26, 2026, and they will put all seven finalists through their paces, trying to decide which of them is worthy of ending up in the final. All jurors will allocate 25 points to the finalists, and they must name a winner in each round of voting, which will get a maximum of 10 points.\nThe jurors can cast their votes until December 31, 2026, as that is when the grand winner will be selected, and it will be kept a secret until the end of the following week, as it will be announced at the 2027 Brussels Motor Show in Belgium on January 8, 2027, which is the same venue as the last three editions of the award.\nThe latest BMW iX3 entered production a little over a year ago in Hungary, and the LWB model is assembled in China. The Neue Klasse electric premium compact crossover is based on the same platform as the modern-day i3, and it is offered in several configurations worldwide.\nCupra’s Raval comes to life in Spain. It is a five-door hatchback that is pretty much a sportier and more premium version of the Volkswagen ID. Polo, as it shares the same MEB+ construction. The architecture is also used by the Skoda Epiq and VW ID. Cross.\nPhoto: European Car of the Year As for the Hyundai Ioniq 3 , it is assembled in Turkey, and wears the design signature of SangYup Lee, the man behind the 2010 Chevy Camaro, Bentley Bentayga (together with Luc Donckerwolke), Genesis GV80, Hyundai N Vision 74 Concept, and others. The model is a subcompact hatch based on on the same platform as the Kia EV2.\nKia’s EV2 is also on the list. It comes to life in Slovakia, features a front-motor and front-wheel drive layout, and is the successor of the Soul EV (e-Soul). The e-GMP (400V) construction is used by the Hyundai Ioniq 3, as mentioned above, as well as the Kia EV3, EV4, and EV5.\nRenault’s modern-day Twingo E-Tech shares its CMF-B EV (AmpR Small) nuts and bolts with the larger 4 E-Tech and 5 E-Tech, as well as the Alpina A290, Nissan Micra, and the latest Dacia Spring. It’s made in Slovenia, and is a city car (A-Segment).\nVolkswagen’s ID. Polo is the latest generation of the popular supermini, and as its name implies, it is a fully-electric vehicle. It’s made in Spain, has a front-motor and front-wheel drive layout, and is based on the same platform as the Cupra Raval and Skoda Epiq, as mentioned above.\nAs for the Volvo EX60 , it utilizes the company’s SPA3 construction, just like the upcoming Polestar 7. It is assembled in Sweden, and has a rear-motor and rear-wheel drive, or a dual-motor and all-wheel drive layout.\nPhoto: European Car of the Year These seven models will fight each other in order to win the 2027 European Car of the Year Award, and as we already told you, the winner will be announced in early January, during the 2027 Brussels Motor Show. Which of these do you think deserves the crown?\n2027 European Car of the Year Finalists BMW iX3\nCupra Raval\nHyundai Ioniq 3\nKia EV2\nRenault Twingo E-Tech\nVolkswagen ID. Polo\nVolvo EX60\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nCar of the Year Cupra Raval Hyundai Ioniq 3 Kia EV2 Renault Twingo E-Tech volkswagen id. polo Volvo EX60 BMW i3\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Cristian Gnaticov\nAfter a series of unfortunate events put an end to Cristian's dream of entering a custom built & tuned old-school Dacia into a rally competition, he moved on to drive press cars and write for a living. He's worked for several automotive online journals and now he's back at autoevolution after his first tour in the mid-2000s. Full profile\nUP NEXT Celebrating Five Iconic Cars That Went Into Production Half a Century Ago",
